 ## Overview
-This method aggregates feature representations from multiple camera views to produce a robust density feature $f_\sigma$ for neural volume rendering. It solves the problem of incomplete geometric information in limited-view scenarios by integrating local view-specific features with global statistical summaries. It differs from its siblings, [ResNet Architecture](8378-resnet-architecture.md) and [CNN-based 2D Feature Extraction](8649-cnn-based-2d-feature-extraction.md), by explicitly modeling inter-view dependencies through weighted pooling rather than relying solely on hierarchical spatial convolutions or residual blocks within a single view.
+This method aggregates 2D features from multiple reference views to predict a volume density feature $f_\sigma$ for a specific 3D point. It solves the problem of integrating multi-perspective information by using a weighted pooling mechanism that combines local view features with global statistical summaries. It differs from its parent, [Encoder and Feature Fusion](7178-encoder-and-feature-fusion.md), by explicitly modeling view importance via attention weights derived from global variance, rather than simple concatenation or averaging. It is distinct from [Feature Fusion for Radiance Decoding](12875-feature-fusion-for-radiance-decoding.md), which typically handles color/appearance, by focusing specifically on the density component $\sigma$ required for volume rendering integration.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A set of 2D feature maps $\{f_i\}_{i=1}^m$ extracted from $m$ different camera views.
-- Output: A density feature vector $f_\sigma$.
